.TextAnnotation
Attributes orient="N0" x=7680 y=480
TextInfo text="Assume the transistor DC SOA is just limited by the allowable power dissipation at our operating voltage\n\nTa = ambient temperature (°C)\nTc = transistor case temperature (°C)\nTj = transistor junction temperature (°C)\nRthjc  = thermal resistance junction to case (°C/W)\nRthca = thermal resistance case to ambient, ie heatsink (°C/W)\nPd = power dissipation (W)\n\nSteady State Equations\nTj = Ta + Pd × (Rthjc + Rthca)\nRthca = (Tj - Ta) ÷ Pd - Rthjc\nTc = Ta + Pd × Rthca\n\nData & calculation\nVsupply max = 16V\nIoutmax = 2.75A\nso Pd = 44W (but not allowing for any tolerances)\nTa allow 30°C\n\nTIP147\nSOA = 125W at Tc = 25°C\nTj allow 125°C (usual to allow a 25°C margin to absolute max)\nRthjc = 1°C/W\nso Rthca = (125 - 30) ÷ 44 - 1 = 1.16°C/W\nTc = 30 + 44 × 1.16 = 81°C\n\nSUP70060E\nSOA = 200W at Tc = 25°C\nTj allow 150°C (usual to allow a 25°C margin to absolute max)\nRthjc = 0.75°C/W\nso Rthca = (150 - 30) ÷ 44 - 0.75 = 1.98°C/W\nTc = 30 + 44 × 1.98 = 117°C\n\nSo the mosfet can have a smaller heatsink but at the expense of running hotter.\nThough it could have the same size heatsink as the darlington but run a cooler Tj"
Property name="StyleNormal" value="DefaultAnnotation" autopos=1 normal="Left" rotated="Left" visible=0 font="Default"
Property name="StyleSelected" value="DefaultSelected" autopos=1 normal="Left" rotated="Left" visible=0 font="Default"
Property name="TextAlignment" value="AlignLeft" autopos=1 normal="Left" rotated="Left" visible=0 font="Default"
.EndTextAnnotation
